    "title": "Best Home Generator for Power Outages",
    "url": "/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-03", modified: "2026-11-03" });
const title = "Home Battery Backup: 7 Whole-House Systems, Installed and Plug-In";
const description =
  "Seven home battery backup systems, from installer-only Powerwall 3 and Enphase to online EcoFlow and Anker kits, with kWh sizing, cost bands and safe wiring.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "tesla-powerwall-3",
    "name": "Tesla Powerwall 3 Home Battery",
    "category": "13.5kWh LFP, 11.5kW, Built-In Solar Inverter",
    "badge": "Best Installed Battery",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "11.5kW continuous output is the highest single-unit rating here",
      "Built-in solar inverter saves a box on new solar installs",
      "DC expansion units add storage without another inverter",
      "Mature app and widely available installer network",
      "Whole-home backup possible with Tesla's backup hardware"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Installer-only; no buying it online",
      "Price set by local installer, often well into five figures",
      "Best value when bought alongside new solar"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The benchmark installed home battery, especially if you are adding solar at the same time.",
    "description": "The Powerwall 3 is what most people picture when they think of a house battery, and its specifications explain why. Tesla rates it at 13.5kWh of usable storage and 11.5kW of continuous output, which is enough in a single unit to run most of a typical home, including many central air conditioners, during an outage. Unlike earlier Powerwalls, it has a solar inverter built in, so a new solar-plus-storage system needs one less piece of equipment, and Tesla's DC expansion units add more storage without adding another inverter. It uses LFP chemistry. You cannot buy it online and install it yourself; it is sold and installed through Tesla and certified installers, who also supply the backup gateway or switch that isolates the house from the grid. That makes the price a quote, not a list number, and it is usually best value when solar is part of the same project."
  },
  {
    "id": "enphase-iq-battery-5p",
    "name": "Enphase IQ Battery 5P Home Battery",
    "category": "5kWh LFP per unit, 3.84kW, Modular",
    "badge": "Best for Enphase Solar",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Modular 5kWh steps let you buy exactly what you need",
      "Natural fit for homes with Enphase microinverters",
      "LFP chemistry with a long warranty",
      "3.84kW continuous and 7.68kW peak per unit, per Enphase",
      "Multiple units add both storage and output"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Installer-only",
      "Needs several units to match one Powerwall",
      "Whole-home backup requires the IQ System Controller"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right battery if your roof already runs on Enphase microinverters.",
    "description": "Enphase builds its battery the way it builds its solar: in small, modular pieces. Each IQ Battery 5P stores 5kWh and is rated by Enphase for 3.84kW of continuous output and 7.68kW of short-term peak, and units stack, so two give you 10kWh and roughly double the output, three give you 15kWh and so on. That makes it easy to size precisely, starting small and adding more later. It is AC-coupled and uses LFP cells, and it pairs most cleanly with homes already running Enphase microinverters, where the whole system lives in one app. Backup requires Enphase's IQ System Controller, which handles the grid isolation. Like the Powerwall, it is sold only through installers. The trade-off for the modularity is that matching a single Powerwall 3 takes several units, and the per-kWh cost can climb as you stack them."
  },
  {
    "id": "franklinwh-apower-2",
    "name": "FranklinWH aPower 2 Home Battery",
    "category": "15kWh LFP, 10kW, aGate Controller",
    "badge": "Best for Generator Pairing",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "15kWh per unit is the largest single battery here",
      "10kW continuous output handles heavy loads",
      "aGate controller has a dedicated generator input",
      "Works alongside existing solar from other brands",
      "LFP chemistry"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Installer-only",
      "Smaller installer network than Tesla or Enphase",
      "Price depends entirely on local quotes"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The installed battery to choose when you also want a generator in the plan.",
    "description": "FranklinWH is the least famous of the three installed brands and arguably the most flexible. The aPower 2 is rated at 15kWh of storage and 10kW of continuous output per unit, putting it in the same class as the Powerwall 3 with a little more capacity per box. The real draw is the aGate, the controller that sits between the battery, the grid, the house and any solar. It is designed to work with solar from other manufacturers and includes a dedicated input for a standby or portable generator, so a battery-plus-generator system can be managed from one box rather than with improvised switching. That makes it a strong choice for rural homes that see long outages and want the generator to step in automatically when the battery runs low. It is sold through certified installers only, so availability and price depend on who serves your area."
  },
  {
    "id": "ecoflow-delta-pro-ultra",
    "name": "EcoFlow Delta Pro Ultra Whole Home Battery Generator",
    "category": "6kWh per Battery, 7.2kW, 120/240V",
    "badge": "Best Plug-In System",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "120/240V split-phase output from one inverter",
      "Scales from 6kWh to 30kWh per inverter, 90kWh stacked, per EcoFlow",
      "High solar input, up to 5.6kW per inverter",
      "Smart Home Panel 2 provides managed circuit backup",
      "Can be bought online and expanded over time"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Battery modules are very heavy",
      "Still needs an electrician and a panel or switch",
      "Expensive once multiple batteries are added"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The closest thing to a Powerwall you can order online, with 240V and room to grow.",
    "description": "The Delta Pro Ultra is EcoFlow's whole-home system and the most capable plug-in option here. It is built as stacking modules: an inverter on top of one or more 6kWh LFP batteries. EcoFlow rates the inverter at 7.2kW continuous with true 120/240V split-phase output, so it can run 240V loads such as a well pump or many central air units, and it states that one inverter supports up to 30kWh of batteries, with stacked inverters reaching 90kWh. Solar input is listed at up to 5.6kW per inverter, which is enough for a serious ground-mounted array. Connection to the house is through EcoFlow's Smart Home Panel 2, which manages circuits automatically, or a conventional transfer switch, either installed by an electrician. The modules are heavy two-person lifts, so this is a garage system that happens to have wheels, not something you carry to a campsite."
  },
  {
    "id": "anker-solix-f3800-plus",
    "name": "Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus Portable Power Station",
    "category": "3.84kWh LFP, 6kW, 120/240V, Expandable",
