    "description": "The Bluetti AC500 is a modular system: a 5,000W inverter head that pairs with separate B300S battery packs, each rated at 3,072Wh of LFP storage. Bluetti states up to six B300S packs per AC500, for about 18.4kWh, so you can start with one or two and add more as needed. Because the inverter and batteries are separate boxes, each piece is lighter and easier to move than a single giant unit, which matters if you have stairs between the garage and the basement. The limitation is voltage: the AC500 is a 120V system, so it cannot run a well pump, electric dryer or central air on its own. Bluetti sells a separate box to combine two units for 240V output, but at that point one of the native 240V systems above is usually the simpler buy. For a gas-heated house on city water, it is a capable, flexible backup."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Home Battery Backup",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-home-battery-backup/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How big a home battery backup do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Add up the running watts of everything that must operate at once and the largest starting surge to size the inverter, usually 3-6kW for essentials. Then estimate daily use, often 5-10kWh for essentials with gas heat. Multiply by the days you want to cover without recharging. Most homes land between 10 and 30kWh of storage for a realistic plan."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a home battery run central air conditioning?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Often, but only with enough output. A single Tesla Powerwall 3 is rated at 11.5kW, which covers many central air units. Plug-in systems need 240V output and enough surge, and some homes need two linked inverters or a soft-start kit on the compressor. Air conditioning also drains a battery quickly, so pair it with solar or plan to run it sparingly."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a battery backup better than a generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A battery is silent, produces no exhaust, switches over almost instantly and needs no fuel or maintenance. A generator runs as long as there is fuel, which matters in long winter outages. For frequent short outages a battery is better. For multi-day outages with little sun, a generator or a battery-plus-generator combination is the more reliable choice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I install a home battery myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can buy a plug-in system online and use it with extension cords on your own. Connecting any battery to your home's wiring requires a transfer switch, interlock or the maker's backup panel, installed by a licensed electrician, and usually a permit. Installed wall batteries like the Powerwall, Enphase and FranklinWH are only sold through certified installers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a home battery last during an outage?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Divide usable capacity by your average load. A 13.5kWh battery running a 500-watt essentials load lasts roughly a day; at 2,000 watts it lasts under seven hours. Inverter losses shave off a little more. Solar recharging can extend that indefinitely in good weather, and a generator can recharge it on cloudy days."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does Generac make a battery backup?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Generac's PWRcell is an installed home battery system sold through Generac dealers, and it is what most people mean by a Generac battery generator. It competes with the Powerwall, Enphase and FranklinWH. Compare it on usable capacity, continuous output, warranty and installer quality, the same terms used for every system in this guide."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do home batteries last before replacement?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most systems here use LFP chemistry, which is rated by manufacturers for thousands of charge cycles. Warranties on installed systems commonly run 10-15 years with a guaranteed remaining capacity. A battery used only for occasional outages will age mainly with time and heat rather than cycles, so a cool, protected installation location helps it last longer."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
