    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A home battery backup is not one product category but two. Wall-mounted batteries are sold and installed by solar contractors; expandable power stations ship in boxes and plug into a transfer switch. Pick the route first and the model almost picks itself.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="two-routes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Installed Batteries vs Plug-In Systems
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every house battery does the same basic job: it stores electricity while the grid is up and feeds it back into some or all of your circuits when the grid goes down. Where products differ is how they get into your house. On one side are permanently installed wall batteries such as the Tesla Powerwall 3, Enphase IQ Battery 5P and FranklinWH aPower 2. On the other are large expandable power stations from EcoFlow, Anker, Jackery and Bluetti that you can order online, roll into a garage and connect through a transfer switch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installed batteries are bought the way you buy a furnace. A certified installer surveys the home, designs the system, pulls the permit, mounts the hardware, wires it to your main panel and registers it with the utility. You get a single warranty, a system that switches over on its own and, usually, deep integration with rooftop solar. You also get an installed price that runs well into five figures and a waiting list that depends on how busy local contractors are.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plug-in systems trade some of that polish for flexibility. The battery and inverter are one portable unit, or a stack of modules on wheels, and you can start with a single battery and add more later. They still need a proper connection to the house, which means an electrician installing a transfer switch, an interlock or the manufacturer's own smart panel, but the battery itself can leave the house for a camping trip or a job site. For renters and people who move often, that portability matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a third group worth naming: the standby battery systems sold by generator brands. Generac's PWRcell line is the obvious example, and it is the reason so many people search for a Generac battery generator. It is an installed system, sold through Generac dealers, and it competes directly with the Powerwall and Enphase. If a Generac dealer is already quoting you a standby generator, ask for a PWRcell quote alongside it, but judge it on the same capacity, output and warranty terms as everything else here.
          </p>

          <p>
            What none of these are is a small portable power station. A 1kWh box will keep a router and a phone alive, and our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a> covers that tier properly. A home battery backup in the sense this guide means starts around 4kWh of storage and a few kilowatts of 240V-capable output, and it is meant to run real household circuits for hours, not charge devices.
          </p>
        </div>

