        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Size a Battery Backup for a House
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sizing comes down to two numbers, and buyers mix them up constantly. Kilowatt-hours (kWh) are the size of the tank: how much energy the battery holds. Kilowatts (kW) are the size of the pipe: how much power the inverter can deliver at any instant. A battery with lots of kWh and a small inverter will run a few things for a very long time. A big inverter on a small battery will run everything at once, briefly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the pipe. List the loads that must run during an outage and add their running watts, then add the largest single starting surge on top. A typical essentials list looks like this: refrigerator around 150 watts running with a much higher start, furnace blower 400-800 watts, lights and internet 200 watts, a sump pump that can spike hard on start, and a well pump if you have one. Most homes land between 3 and 6kW of continuous need for essentials, and 8-12kW once central air enters the picture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then size the tank. A modern refrigerator might use 1-2kWh a day, a gas furnace blower a few kWh on a cold day, and lights, internet and phones another 1-2kWh. An essentials-only house therefore uses somewhere around 5-10kWh a day during an outage if everyone is careful. Add an electric water heater, electric cooking or air conditioning and that figure climbs to 20-40kWh a day, which is where battery-only backup gets expensive fast.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Rough Daily Use During an Outage</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5-10kWh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Essentials only, gas heat</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">15-25kWh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Essentials plus well pump and some AC</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">30kWh+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Most of the house, electric heat or AC</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3-6kW</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical essentials inverter need</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Decide how many days you want to cover without recharging. One day of essentials is a realistic target for a single Powerwall 3, a two-battery Delta Pro Ultra or a well-specified Explorer 5000 Plus. Three days requires either a lot of batteries or a way to refill them, which is why solar, and in some homes a small generator, matter so much in the next sections. If you want the essentials-only version of this calculation at a smaller scale, our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">best portable power station for home backup</a> runs the same math on 1-4kWh units.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, size for the loads you will actually ration. Every system on this list lets you choose which circuits are backed up, either through a critical-loads subpanel or a smart panel with switchable breakers. Backing up eight circuits you genuinely need is far cheaper than backing up the whole panel and hoping nobody turns on the dryer. The cheapest kilowatt-hour is the one you decided you could live without.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="connecting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Connecting a Battery to Your House Safely
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the section that matters most, because a home battery connected the wrong way can kill a lineworker. During an outage, any power you push back into the utility lines is stepped up by the transformer outside your house to thousands of volts. That is backfeeding, and the only safe way to prevent it is equipment that physically separates your house from the grid before the battery powers it. A so-called suicide cord, a male-to-male plug run into a dryer outlet, is illegal, dangerous and has no place in any backup plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installed batteries handle isolation as part of the system. The Powerwall 3 works with Tesla's Backup Gateway or Backup Switch, Enphase uses the IQ System Controller, and FranklinWH uses the aGate. Each of these detects the outage, opens the connection to the grid and then lets the battery energize your circuits, usually fast enough that clocks do not reset. The installer sizes and installs that hardware, pulls the permit and arranges the inspection, and the utility is involved because the system may also export solar power.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plug-in systems need the same isolation, provided by different hardware. The options are a manual transfer switch for selected circuits, a generator interlock kit on the main panel, or the maker's own backup panel: EcoFlow's Smart Home Panel 2, Anker's Home Power Panel and Jackery's Smart Transfer Switch are all built for this. Whichever you choose, a licensed electrician should install it, and in most jurisdictions a permit is required. The battery itself plugs into an inlet box on the outside or garage wall, exactly as a portable generator would.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No transfer switch, no connection to the house.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A battery or generator may only feed house wiring through a listed transfer switch, an approved interlock or the manufacturer's isolation hardware, installed by a licensed electrician. Anything else risks backfeeding the grid, damaging equipment and electrocuting utility workers.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are not ready for an electrician, you can still use a plug-in system the way you would use a portable generator: run heavy-duty extension cords from the unit's outlets to the fridge, a lamp and the internet gear. That is safe as long as nothing connects to the house wiring. It is clumsy, and it will not run hardwired loads like a furnace or well pump, but it is a legitimate first step.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more protection worth adding with any battery install is surge protection at the panel. Grid restoration after a storm is when voltage spikes are most likely, and a battery inverter is an expensive thing to fry. Our guide to the <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">best whole-house surge protector</a> covers the panel-mounted devices an electrician can add during the same visit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recharging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Solar, Recharging and Long Outages
          </h2>

          <p>
            A battery without a way to recharge is a countdown timer. For an outage of a few hours that is fine; for a multi-day storm or a wildfire shutoff, what decides whether the lights stay on is how fast the battery can be refilled. There are three sources: the grid when it returns, solar panels, and a fuel generator. Most serious home battery backup plans use at least two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installed batteries are usually designed around rooftop solar. The Powerwall 3 has a solar inverter built in, so a new solar-plus-battery install needs one less box. Enphase batteries are AC-coupled and pair naturally with Enphase microinverter systems, and FranklinWH's aGate can work alongside existing solar from other makers. With a properly sized roof array, a house in a sunny climate can refill much of a day's essential use on a clear day, which turns a one-day battery into an indefinite one as long as the weather cooperates.
          </p>

          <p>
            The plug-in systems accept solar directly, usually through high-voltage inputs designed for portable or ground-mounted panels. EcoFlow quotes up to 5.6kW of solar input for a Delta Pro Ultra inverter, and the Anker, Jackery and Bluetti units all accept several kilowatts. In practice the limit is where you can put the panels. A row of panels on a ground rack in the backyard can refill a few kWh a day; folding panels laid out on a deck can add a meaningful top-up. Our guides to the <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">best portable solar panels</a> and the <a href="/best-solar-generator/">best solar generators</a> cover how to match panel wattage to an input window.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pairing a battery with a generator is the most reliable answer for long outages in cloudy or winter conditions. The generator runs for a few hours to refill the battery at its most efficient load, then shuts off while the battery carries the house quietly overnight. The aGate has a dedicated generator input, and the plug-in systems can charge from a generator through their AC inputs. If you go this route, an inverter generator is the better match, and our <a href="/best-small-generator-for-home/">small generator for home</a> guide lists ones that suit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Expect batteries to recharge more slowly than their output suggests. Charge rates from the wall are often limited by circuit size, solar harvest is limited by sun and panel angle, and cold batteries charge slowly or not at all until they warm up. LFP chemistry, used by most of the systems here, is safe and long-lived but dislikes charging below freezing, which is why units installed in unheated garages in cold climates should have heating or be placed somewhere protected.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cost, Incentives and Permits
          </h2>

          <p>
            Prices move too quickly for exact figures, but the bands are stable enough to plan around. An installed wall battery like a single Powerwall 3 or FranklinWH aPower 2 commonly lands somewhere in the low-to-mid teens of thousands of dollars installed, and more if your panel needs an upgrade or the install is complicated. Enphase systems scale in 5kWh steps, so the price depends heavily on how many units you need.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plug-in systems start lower and climb with every battery added. A single Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus or Jackery Explorer 5000 Plus is a few thousand dollars, a Delta Pro Ultra with one battery somewhat more, and the electrician's work for an inlet and transfer switch or smart panel adds a further sum that varies a great deal by region. By the time a plug-in system matches the capacity of a Powerwall, the gap between the two routes has narrowed considerably.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not assume the federal tax credit applies. The 30 percent federal residential clean energy credit that covered home batteries for several years was ended for systems paid for after December 31, 2025, under the 2025 federal tax law. Some states, utilities and local programs still offer rebates or pay homeowners to let the utility draw on their battery during peak demand, and those can be worth real money. Ask any installer to show which incentives are current in your area rather than quoting old numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Permits are part of the price, not an optional extra. Installed systems always go through permitting and usually a utility interconnection agreement if solar is involved. Plug-in systems need a permit for the transfer switch or panel work in most places, even though the battery itself is a consumer product. Insurers and future buyers of your house will both care whether that work was inspected, so it is worth doing properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Operating costs are low. There is no fuel, no oil change and no weekly exercise cycle. The main long-term cost is battery degradation, and most of the systems here are rated for thousands of cycles and backed by long warranties. If you want a broader cost comparison against a fuel-burning standby unit, our <a href="/generac-generator-cost/">Generac generator cost</a> breakdown covers the installed price of the generator side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-generator" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Battery Backup vs a Standby Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            Home backup electrical generators and home batteries solve the same problem in opposite ways. A standby generator burns natural gas or propane, so it runs as long as the fuel lasts, which for a home on a gas line means essentially indefinitely. A battery runs for as long as its stored energy lasts, then needs refilling. If your outages routinely run for days in winter, that difference decides the question.
          </p>

          <p>
            Batteries win on almost everything else. They are silent, so neighbors and noise ordinances are not a concern. They produce no exhaust, so there is no carbon monoxide risk and no clearance from windows to worry about. They switch over so fast that most electronics never notice, while a standby generator takes several seconds to start and transfer. And they earn their keep every day if paired with solar or a time-of-use rate plan, while a generator sits idle until the next storm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Generators win on surge and duration per dollar. A whole-house standby generator rated for 20kW or more can start a central air conditioner, a well pump and an electric range without blinking, and a buried propane tank can run it for days. Matching that with batteries takes several high-output units and a lot of storage. Our guide to the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generator for power outages</a> covers the fuel side if that is the better fit, and the <a href="/best-gas-generator/">best gas generator</a> guide covers portable options.
          </p>

          <p>
            For many homes the right answer is both. A modest battery carries the house through the frequent short outages silently and instantly, and a generator, portable or standby, covers the rare long ones by recharging the battery for a few hours a day. That arrangement also lets a smaller, cheaper generator do the work of a big one, because it never has to handle the starting surge of every appliance at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you only lose power a few times a year for a few hours, a battery alone is the cleaner solution. If you lose it for days every winter and have no sun to speak of, a generator or a generator-plus-battery setup is the honest recommendation, and spending a fortune on batteries alone would be the wrong call.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Home Battery Backup to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you are installing solar anyway, or already have it, get quotes for the <strong>Tesla Powerwall 3</strong> and the <strong>FranklinWH aPower 2</strong>. Both offer a lot of storage and output in one unit, and the Powerwall's built-in solar inverter makes it especially efficient for new solar installs. If you already have Enphase microinverters, the <strong>Enphase IQ Battery 5P</strong> is the natural fit, and its 5kWh steps let you buy exactly as much as you need.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a whole home backup power solution without a contractor-led solar project, the <strong>EcoFlow Delta Pro Ultra</strong> is the most capable plug-in system here. Its 240V output and high solar input make it the closest thing to a Powerwall you can order online, and it scales from one battery to a garage full. Our <a href="/best-ecoflow-power-station/">EcoFlow power station guide</a> covers the smaller models if you want to stay in that ecosystem at a lower budget.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want the most capacity per dollar to start, look at the <strong>Jackery Explorer 5000 Plus</strong>, which puts 5kWh and 240V output in one wheeled unit. If you are already shopping for an Anker battery backup, the <strong>Anker SOLIX F3800 Plus</strong> is the pick: 240V, 6kW and expansion batteries that slot in later. Our <a href="/best-anker-solix-power-station/">Anker SOLIX power station</a> and <a href="/best-jackery-power-station/">Jackery power station</a> guides cover each lineup in more depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            The <strong>Bluetti AC500 with B300S batteries</strong> is for homes where 120V circuits are enough: gas heat, gas water heater, city water, no central air. Its modular batteries are easy to add, and without the 240V hardware it is often cheaper per kilowatt-hour. If you need 240V for a well pump or dryer, skip it and buy one of the others.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if none of this fits the budget, do not buy a small, undersized home battery just to say you have one. A good 1-2kWh portable power station for the fridge and internet, plus a few <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">portable chargers</a> and lanterns, will get most households through a typical outage, and you can save toward a real system later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding through a dryer outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plugging a battery or generator into a household outlet with a double-male cord energizes the utility lines and can kill a lineworker. It can also start a fire in your own walls. Every connection to house wiring must go through a transfer switch, interlock or the maker's isolation hardware, installed by a licensed electrician.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing by kWh and ignoring kW</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A big battery with a small inverter cannot start a well pump or central air. List the loads that must run at the same time, add the largest starting surge, and buy an inverter that covers it before worrying about how many kilowatt-hours you need. Runtime is easy to add later; output often is not.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming old tax credit numbers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many sales pitches still quote the 30 percent federal credit, which ended for home battery systems paid for after 2025. Ask the installer which state, utility or local incentives are current in your area and get them in writing. A quote that depends on an expired credit is not a real quote.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backing up the whole panel by default</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whole-panel backup sounds best, but it lets anyone switch on a dryer or electric oven and drain the battery in an hour. A critical-loads subpanel or a smart panel with selected circuits stretches the same battery much further. Decide what you can live without before you pay to back it up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting LFP batteries in a freezing garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                LFP chemistry is safe and long-lived but cannot charge normally below freezing, and some units will refuse to charge until they warm up. In cold climates, install the battery in a conditioned space or choose a model with built-in heating, and check the manufacturer's stated operating temperature range before picking a spot.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

