        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big a home battery backup do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Add up the running watts of everything that must operate at once and the largest starting surge to size the inverter, usually 3-6kW for essentials. Then estimate daily use, often 5-10kWh for essentials with gas heat. Multiply by the days you want to cover without recharging. Most homes land between 10 and 30kWh of storage for a realistic plan.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a home battery run central air conditioning?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, but only with enough output. A single Tesla Powerwall 3 is rated at 11.5kW, which covers many central air units. Plug-in systems need 240V output and enough surge, and some homes need two linked inverters or a soft-start kit on the compressor. Air conditioning also drains a battery quickly, so pair it with solar or plan to run it sparingly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a battery backup better than a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A battery is silent, produces no exhaust, switches over almost instantly and needs no fuel or maintenance. A generator runs as long as there is fuel, which matters in long winter outages. For frequent short outages a battery is better. For multi-day outages with little sun, a generator or a battery-plus-generator combination is the more reliable choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a home battery myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can buy a plug-in system online and use it with extension cords on your own. Connecting any battery to your home's wiring requires a transfer switch, interlock or the maker's backup panel, installed by a licensed electrician, and usually a permit. Installed wall batteries like the Powerwall, Enphase and FranklinWH are only sold through certified installers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a home battery last during an outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide usable capacity by your average load. A 13.5kWh battery running a 500-watt essentials load lasts roughly a day; at 2,000 watts it lasts under seven hours. Inverter losses shave off a little more. Solar recharging can extend that indefinitely in good weather, and a generator can recharge it on cloudy days.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Generac make a battery backup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Generac's PWRcell is an installed home battery system sold through Generac dealers, and it is what most people mean by a Generac battery generator. It competes with the Powerwall, Enphase and FranklinWH. Compare it on usable capacity, continuous output, warranty and installer quality, the same terms used for every system in this guide.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do home batteries last before replacement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most systems here use LFP chemistry, which is rated by manufacturers for thousands of charge cycles. Warranties on installed systems commonly run 10-15 years with a guaranteed remaining capacity. A battery used only for occasional outages will age mainly with time and heat rather than cycles, so a cool, protected installation location helps it last longer.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
